# Trivy ignore file — reviewed, accepted risks ONLY.
#
# POLICY (unchanged since ADR 0004): an entry here requires a written
# justification of why the finding does not apply, or why the risk is
# deliberately accepted, plus a review date. "We will fix it later" is not a
# justification. A transport error (e.g. an HTTP 429 from a package registry) is
# NOT a finding and must never be silenced here — fix the scan instead.
#
# REVIEW DATE: 2026-02-28 (6 months).
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AVD-AWS-0107 — ingress from the public internet
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fires on the HTTP (80) and HTTPS (443) ingress rules in infra/network.tf.
#
# This is a PUBLIC WEB APPLICATION. nginx terminates traffic on 80/443 and the
# platform's own verify stage health-checks the service over the public IP.
# Ports 80 and 443 being world-reachable is the product requirement; a check
# that flags "0.0.0.0/0 ingress" cannot distinguish an intentionally public web
# port from an accidentally exposed admin port.
#
# WHAT WAS ACTUALLY FIXED RATHER THAN IGNORED: the same check also fired on SSH
# (port 22), which was open to 0.0.0.0/0. That one was a real misconfiguration
# and it has been FIXED in code — port 22 is now restricted to GitHub's
# published Actions runner CIDR ranges (see the `app_ssh` rule and the
# `github_meta` data source in infra/network.tf). Only the deliberately public
# web ports are ignored below.
#
# Residual risk accepted: the application itself is the security boundary on
# 80/443. That boundary is tested by the JWT authentication suite, the Semgrep
# SAST gate and the smoke checks (unauthenticated request -> 401, forged token
# -> 401).
#
# Revisit if: a load balancer or CDN is introduced (terminate there and close
# the instance ports), or the service stops being publicly reachable.
AVD-AWS-0107
